(full desktop monitors and uses 'max-width' queries to hide, shrink, or override elements) Tj
T*
(for smaller devices. Mobile-first is superior because mobile devices possess constrained) Tj
T*
(bandwidth, memory, and CPU: downloading heavy desktop styles only to override them) Tj
T*
(degrades performance.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the internal mathematical mechanics of the Bootstrap 12-column) Tj
T*
(responsive grid system.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Bootstrap's grid is built on Flexbox. A horizontal container row \(.row\) has) Tj
T*
(negative margins \(-12px\) that cancel out the horizontal padding of its parent container.) Tj
T*
(Child columns \(.col-*\) have 12px of padding on each side \(creating 24px gutters\). The) Tj
T*
(12-column system is based on fractional math: each column class represents a fraction of) Tj
T*
(12. For instance, .col-4 occupies 4/12 = 33.333% width; .col-6 occupies 6/12 = 50%;) Tj
T*
(.col-12 occupies 100%. Responsive infix classes \(.col-md-6\) apply flex-basis: 50% only) Tj
T*
(when the viewport crosses the medium breakpoint \(768px\), defaulting to 100% width on) Tj
T*
(smaller mobile screens.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 3.6 Key Takeaways and Revision Points) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Responsive design combines Fluid Grids, Flexible Images, and Media Queries.) Tj
T*
(* The viewport meta tag is mandatory to prevent mobile browser canvas scaling.) Tj
T*
(* Mobile-first architecture progressively enhances layout via min-width queries.) Tj
T*
(* Bootstrap divides horizontal space into 12 flexible fractional columns.) Tj
T*
